.\" Manual page for ncpatcher.
.TH NCPATCHER 1 "September 1, 2026" "ncpatcher" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ncpatcher \- compile code and splice it into a Nintendo DS ROM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ncpatcher
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ncpatcher
compiles C, C++ and assembly with a cross-toolchain and patches the result into
a Nintendo DS ROM's ARM binaries and overlays. What it builds, and where it
writes it, is described by
.B ncpatcher.yaml
in the project directory.
.PP
With no command it prints its version and where to find the help, and does
nothing else. Building is asked for by name.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR build " [" \-\-variant " " \fINAME\fP " | " \-\-all\-variants "]"
Compile and patch. Select one configured variant, or build every variant in
declaration order.
.TP
.BR init " [" \-\-template " " \fINAME\fP "]"
Create a version 2 project in the directory selected by
.BR \-C ,
or in the current directory when it is omitted. The templates are
.B default
and
.BR nsmb .
Both create
.BR ncpatcher.yaml ,
.B source/
and
.BR include/ ,
and refuse to overwrite an existing configuration.
.TP
.BR clean " [" \-\-backups ]
Delete the build directories. With
.BR \-\-backups ,
also delete the backup directory \(em which leaves the ROM binaries patched with
no way back, so
.B restore
is usually what was meant.
.TP
.B restore
Copy the pristine binaries out of the backup directory back into the ROM
directory, then remove the backups.
.TP
.BR "config dump" " [" \-\-explain ] " [" \-\-json ]
Print every setting after inheritance, variables and globs have been resolved:
what the build would actually use, not what the file says.
.B \-\-explain
adds where each setting came from.
.IP
.B \-\-json
writes an
.B ncpatcher.config/1
document with every path already resolved against the project directory. Besides
the targets and their flags it carries
.B rom\-banner
at the root, and
.B banner
and
.B module\-variants
per variant \(em what a consumer needs to reach the same answers this program
does without reading
.B ncpatcher.yaml
itself. All three keys are always present, so nothing has to test for a key to
learn a project or a variant overrides nothing.
.TP
.B config validate
Load and resolve the configuration, reporting problems, without building.
.TP
.B config path
Print the path of the configuration file that would be used.
.TP
.BR migrate " [" \-\-write ]
Convert a version 1
.B ncpatcher.json
into a commented
.BR ncpatcher.yaml ,
hoisting shared flags and extracting
.B \-D
flags into a
.B defines
list. Prints the result;
.B \-\-write
saves it.
.TP
.B modules list
Show which modules are enabled and what they contribute.
.TP
.BR "modules dump" " [" \-o " " \fIPATH\fP " ]"
Print the resolved module graph as JSON. With
.BR \-o ,
write it to
.I PATH
instead of standard output.
.TP
.BI "modules explain" " NAME"
Explain why a module or component is enabled or disabled and where its target
came from. A component name has the form
.IR Module.Component .
.TP
.B rom info
Print what the ROM header says: the title, where each binary lives, how many
overlays there are, and whether the header checksum matches its contents.
.TP
.BR "rom files" " [" \-\-json ]
List the ROM's NitroFS files: id, size and path, one per line. With
.BR \-\-json ,
print the same table as an
.B ncpatcher.files/1
document. This reads a ROM rather than building one, so every entry is
.B unchanged
and there is no variant.
.IP
Both
.B rom info
and
.B rom files
read the project's ROM, or whichever one
.B \-\-rom
names. They write to standard output and leave the log on standard error, so
.B \-\-json
can be piped into a parser directly.
.TP
.BR "rom extract" " " \fIDIR\fP " [" \-\-code\-only "] [" \-\-decompress\-overlays ]
Write the ROM into
.IR DIR ,
in the layout the patcher reads: the header, the two ARM binaries, the overlay
tables and overlays, the name and allocation tables, the banner, and the NitroFS
tree below the layout's data directory. That set is chosen so the directory is
one
.B ncpatcher
can be pointed straight back at, with
.BR "rom info" ,
.B "rom files"
or
.BR "rom: dir:" .
.IP
An
.B extraction.json
is written alongside, recording the layout used and, per overlay, the form it was
stored in. It is what
.B rom pack
reads to put the original form back.
.IP
.B \-\-code\-only
writes just the binaries, which is not a directory a tool can read back as a
whole ROM.
.IP
Overlays are written exactly as the ROM stores them, compression and all, and a
plain extract and pack gives the ROM back byte for byte.
.B \-\-decompress\-overlays
unpacks them and clears the compression flag in the emitted overlay table to
match; the two only make sense together, since decompressed bytes under a row
still claiming a compressed size is what turns a naive repack into a ROM that
hangs.
.TP
.BI "rom pack" " DIR"
Read
.I DIR
back and write it into the ROM. Files the directory does not have are left as
the ROM had them, so a directory holding only
.B arm9.bin
updates only ARM9. An
.B extraction.json
in the directory is read for the form each overlay was stored in, so an overlay
the extraction unpacked is compressed again and gets its flags back. That round
trip is faithful rather than byte-identical.
.TP
.BR "files plan" " [" \-\-json "] [" \-o " " \fIPATH\fP ]
Report what a build would place in the ROM's file table, without building.
Nothing is written to the ROM or to an extracted directory, and no toolchain is
needed.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-json ,
the output is an
.B ncpatcher.files/1
document with
.B "planned: true"
at the root, so a consumer can never mistake a prospective id for a settled one.
Ids under
.B z_new/
in one are predictions that a build confirms; the provenance fields mean what
they mean after a build.
.IP
The prediction comes from running the real insertion pass against a ROM accessor
that holds every write in memory, so a plan and the build that confirms it are
produced by the same code. A build reads its NitroFS sources, and a project may
generate some of them in a
.B pre\-build
hook, which a plan does not run; such a destination is still planned, with its
id and provenance, and its entry carries
.B "source\-missing: true"
because its size is the one thing that cannot be known.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-C " PATH" "\fR, \fP" \-\-project " PATH"
Project directory, or the configuration file itself. Removes the need to launch
the program from the project directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-rom " PATH"
The ROM to patch, overriding the configuration. Either a
.B .nds
or a directory holding an extracted one; which it is is decided by looking at
the path.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " PATH"
Write the patched ROM here instead of patching it in place. Only meaningful for
a
.B .nds
\(em an extracted directory is patched where it is.
.TP
.BI \-D " NAME[=VAL]" "\fR, \fP" \-\-define " NAME[=VAL]"
Define a preprocessor macro for every translation unit.
.TP
.BI \-\-var " NAME=VAL"
Set a configuration variable, overriding
.B vars:
in the project file. Lets one committed configuration serve a machine whose
reference tree lives somewhere unusual.
.TP
.BI \-\-toolchain " PREFIX"
Cross-compiler prefix, for example
.BR arm-none-eabi- .
.TP
.BI \-j " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-jobs " N"
Compile jobs to run at once. 0 uses one per hardware thread.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable every category of verbose output.
.TP
.BI \-\-verbose-tag " TAG"
Enable one category: build, section, elf, patch, library, linking, symbols,
nolib, all.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " auto|always|never"
When to style the console output.
.B NO_COLOR
in the environment is honored.
.TP
.BI \-\-message-format " human|json"
.B json
emits one JSON object per line on stdout and moves the human log to stderr, so
that a caller has exactly one thing to parse.
.TP
.BI \-\-result " PATH"
Write a JSON summary of the run \(em status, counts, diagnostics and the
artifacts written \(em to this file.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " PATH"
Write the log file here instead of the default
.IR <buildDir>/ncpatcher.log .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-log
Do not write a log file.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-env\-file
Ignore the project's
.IR .ncpatcher.env .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the built-in help.
.SH ENVIRONMENT FILE
A project may carry a
.I .ncpatcher.env
beside its configuration.
.B ${env.NAME}
reads it before the real environment. The format is
.IR NAME=VALUE ,
one per line, with
.B #
comments and optional surrounding quotes; there is no expansion, no
substitution and no
.B export
keyword.
.PP
These entries
.B override
the ambient environment, which is the opposite of the usual convention and is
deliberate: an environment variable belongs to a shell, and a stale value left
in a profile is exactly what this exists to prevent. The command line still
outranks the file. Hook children inherit these entries, though a hook's own
.B env
mapping wins. Use
.B \-\-no\-env\-file
to ignore the file.
.SH BUILD HOOKS
Version 2 projects declare named commands in one
.B hooks:
list. Each entry has a required
.BR name ,
.BR run ,
and
.B when
field. The phase is
.BR pre-build ,
.B post-files
or
.BR post-build .
.B post-files
runs after NitroFS insertion and before compilation, which is the only point at
which a generator can see the file ids this build assigned and still have its
output compiled.
The optional
.B cwd
is relative to the project directory and defaults to it. The optional
.B env
mapping overrides variables for the child only; the rest of the environment is
inherited.
.PP
Hook fields use the normal
.B ${...}
configuration expansion. When
.B modules.dump
is configured,
.B ${ncp.moduleDump}
is its absolute path; likewise
.B ${ncp.fileDump}
for
.BR files\-dump .
The module graph is written before pre-build hooks run, and the file manifest
between NitroFS insertion and the post-files hooks.
.PP
.B ${variant.name}
and
.B ${rom.output}
are resolved when the hook runs rather than when the configuration is read,
because neither is known any earlier. They name the variant being built and the
ROM this build wrote, including the suffix
.B \-\-all\-variants
derives.
.PP
The older
.B pre-build
and
.B post-build
string arrays remain accepted as compatibility syntax, but cannot be combined
with
.B hooks:
in one version 2 file.
.SH NITROFS FILES
The version 2
.B files:
mapping copies loose source files into NitroFS after pre-build hooks and before
compilation. Each key is a slash-separated path inside the ROM; each value is a
source path relative to the project directory. A pre-build hook may generate a
source before it is read.
.PP
A destination outside
.B z_new/
must already exist. Missing paths below
.B z_new/
are appended to the FNT and FAT. Existing paths are replaced.
.PP
Direct
.B .nds
input supports both operations. An extracted layout must include its FNT, FAT,
and data directory.
.PP
An entry may instead be a mapping of
.B source:
and
.BR id: ,
which claims an existing file id: the named file keeps its number and is
renamed to this entry's path. That is how a project gets a path the retail ROM
never had without appending an id at the end of the table. The id must already
exist and must live in the destination's parent directory, since a file id
belongs to its directory's consecutive range and can be renamed but not moved.
Two entries may not claim one id.
.PP
A key may name a file inside a Nitro archive instead of a loose file, by
writing the archive's ROM path, a
.BR ! ,
then the path within the archive:
.BR "ARCHIVE/menu_title.narc!menu/title/USA/vs.bmg" .
Archives are edited in place and never added to, because game code reads a
member by its file id, assigned by the archive's own name table. The archive
must exist and must already hold the member; a missing member is an error
rather than a warning. A replacement of a different size relays the allocation
table and data chunk out around it, and every member keeps its id. An archive nothing edited is written back byte
for byte.
.B id:
cannot be combined with an archive destination.
.PP
Some games store their archives compressed \(em Mario Kart DS stores 286 of
them and names them
.B .carc
\(em and those are edited the same way. An archive is recognised by its bytes
rather than by its extension: the test is whether it decompresses to something
beginning with
.BR NARC ,
so a game shipping compressed archives named
.B .narc
needs no special case, and a file that merely begins with the compression
header byte is not mistaken for one. A container goes back in the wrapper it
arrived in, even where compressing it makes it larger, because a game that reads
an archive through its decompressor will not accept a raw one in its place. The
later LZ11 form is read but not written, and editing such an archive is refused
rather than silently rewritten as LZ10, which the game would decode with the
wrong routine.
.SH RESERVED FILE ID
.B files\-reserve:
names a
.B z_new/
path created as an empty file before any other file the build adds, so that it
is given the first new file id and nothing loadable is.
.PP
Some games hold arrays of file ids in compiled code, ended by a sentinel value,
and the sentinel a compiler picked is the id one past the last file the retail
ROM shipped with \(em exactly the id the first added file would otherwise get.
Whether a game does this, and which id it is, is a fact about the game rather
than about NitroFS, so nothing is reserved unless this key names it. The path
may not also appear in
.B files:
or be supplied by a
.BR file\-trees: entry,
because the placeholder has to stay empty.
.PP
New Super Mario Bros. needs
.BR z_new/reserved ,
which is what
.B init \-\-template nsmb
writes. An existing NSMB project that adds
.B z_new/
files must set the key: without it every one of those files shifts down by one
id.
.SH ROM BANNER
.B rom.banner:
replaces the icon and title the console's menu shows. The banner is not a
NitroFS file but a region of its own that the header points at, so it has its
own key rather than a path in
.BR files: .
.PP
The replacement must be exactly as long as the ROM's own banner: a banner's
length is fixed by the version word it starts with, so a different length is a
different format rather than a bigger banner, and the build refuses instead of
laying the container out again. A variant may override it with its own
.BR banner: ,
though one banner normally serves every build \(em the region carries a title
in all six console languages at once.
.SH NITROFS TREES
The version 2
.B file\-trees:
list sweeps whole directories into NitroFS instead of naming files one at a
time. Each entry takes a
.BR dir: ,
optionally
.B layered:
to read the first path segment as a variant name,
.B base\-variant:
for the layer applied underneath the built variant, and
.B into:
for a ROM path prefix. A module declares its own the same way with
.B nitrofs:
in its
.BR module.yaml .
.PP
Two rules decide a destination more than one tree provides. Layering comes
first: a file chosen for the built variant outranks one that applies to every
variant. Module order comes second: within one layer the first tree to claim a
destination keeps it, in
.B modules.enabled
order. An explicit
.B files:
entry overrides anything a tree swept.
.PP
A module may name its tree layers differently from the way the project names
its variants. A variant entry's
.B module\-variants:
mapping connects the two: keys are module names, values are that module's own
layer name. Only the variant layer is redirected; the module's
.B base\-variant:
is unaffected. A layer named there must exist, unlike an unmapped one, because
naming it is an assertion and a silent fallback to the base layer would ship a
build without its translations.
.PP
A component's
.B files:
patterns name the ROM destinations it owns. Enabled, they record ownership;
disabled, they subtract those files from the component's own module tree, so
switching a feature off removes its assets with its code.
.PP
A tree spells an archive as a directory, since a directory cannot also be a
file: a folder named
.I stem_ext
means the archive
.I stem.ext
beside it, and everything below it is a path inside that archive. So
.B ARCHIVE/menu_title_narc/menu/title/USA/vs.bmg
replaces that member of
.BR ARCHIVE/menu_title.narc ,
and
.B data/Main2D_carc/menu/icon.NCGR
reaches inside a compressed one.
.PP
A segment is read that way only when the ROM actually holds an archive at the
path it names, and is an ordinary directory name otherwise \(em which is why
.B z_new/
is not the archive
.BR z.new ,
and why a ROM that genuinely holds a directory named
.B *_narc
simply works. Only the outermost matching directory is read as an archive.
.SH FILE MANIFEST
.B files\-dump
names a path to write the ROM's file table to, as
.B ncpatcher.files/1
JSON. It is written after NitroFS insertion and before the post-files hooks,
which is the only point at which the ids are settled and the code referring to
them has not yet been compiled.
.B ${ncp.fileDump}
expands to its absolute path, so a hook that turns file ids into constants can
be handed it.
.PP
Every file in the ROM is listed, not only the ones the build wrote: a generator
naming files by id needs the whole table, and most of it is whatever the retail
ROM already had. Each entry carries its
.BR id ,
.BR path ,
.B size
and an
.B action
of
.BR unchanged ,
.B modified
or
.BR created .
A file the build wrote also carries its
.BR source ,
and the
.BR module ,
.B component
and
.B from\-variant
that supplied it \(em which is what tells an editor whether an entry is
vanilla, replaced by a module, or added by one.
.PP
A Nitro archive the build edited also carries a
.B members
array, one entry per member it replaced, each with the member's
.B id
\(em a file id in the archive's own table rather than the ROM's, which is how
game code loads it \(em and its own provenance. The
archive's own entry can carry no more than its members agree on, which for five
members from three modules is nothing, so without this the per-member truth
would be lost. Members the build did not touch are not listed: a consumer with
the ROM open can list them itself.
.PP
Ids are reported raw, exactly as the FAT stores them. A game that offsets file
ids at run time applies that itself. Existing ids are never renumbered, so
.B created
happens only under
.BR z_new/ .
It is a top-level key rather than
.BR files.dump ,
because
.B files:
is a mapping of ROM paths and a ROM may contain a file called
.BR dump .
.SH BUILD VARIANTS
The version 2
.B variants:
mapping names builds that differ only in preprocessor
.B defines
and NitroFS
.BR files .
Project files form the base mapping; a variant file with the same NitroFS
destination replaces its source. Command-line
.B \-D
definitions come after variant definitions.
.PP
.BI "ncpatcher build \-\-variant " NAME
builds one entry. A project that declares variants requires an explicit
selection.
.B ncpatcher build \-\-all\-variants
builds every entry from the original
.BR rom.file .
Each output adds
.BI _ NAME
before the base output extension. For example,
.B build/game.nds
becomes
.BR build/game_en.nds .
Without a configured output, the input ROM name is the base. Building all
variants is unavailable for an extracted directory because it has only one
in-place destination.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Internal error.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.TP
.B 3
The configuration could not be loaded or resolved.
.TP
.B 4
A module could not be resolved.
.TP
.B 5
The toolchain is missing or unusable.
.TP
.B 6
Compilation failed.
.TP
.B 7
Linking failed.
.TP
.B 8
Patching failed.
.TP
.B 9
The ROM could not be read or written.
.TP
.B 10
A hook failed.
.TP
.B 11
Cancelled. Send
.B SIGINT
to the process group to ask for this; compiler children are in that group and
stop with it. The build stops at its next checkpoint \(em between phases, and
between compilations \(em and still writes its
.B \-\-result
document, with
.BR "status: \(dqcancelled\(dq" .
Stopping is safe: the backup store holds the pristine binaries and the
.B .nds
backend writes nothing until the end, so the next run produces the ROM the
interrupted one would have.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NCPATCHER_TOOLCHAIN
Cross-compiler prefix, used when the command line does not say.
.TP
.B NCPATCHER_JOBS
Compile jobs, used when the command line does not say.
.TP
.B NCPATCHER_LOG
Log file path, used when the command line does not say.
.TP
.B NCPATCHER_DATA_DIR
Directory holding
.BR ncp.h ", " ncp_ide.h " and " ncprt.c .
Searched before the install location, for development trees and images that
place them somewhere of their own choosing.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, the console output is not styled.
.PP
Precedence throughout is command line, then environment, then the project file,
then the built-in default.
.B ncpatcher config dump \-\-explain
says which of them won.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ncpatcher.yaml
The project configuration. An
.I ncpatcher.json
is read as the older version 1 schema, which stays supported.
.TP
.I <buildDir>/ncpatcher.log
The log of the last build.
.TP
.I <dataDir>/ncp.h
Force-included into every translation unit. Looked for in
.BR $NCPATCHER_DATA_DIR ,
then
.I <exeDir>/../share/ncpatcher
, then next to the executable.
.SH SEE ALSO
The project README, installed alongside this page, documents the configuration
schema in full.
